  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery shows a deep low over the PacNW and \nHurricane Odalys strengthening to the southwest of Baja. Weak \nridging remains over SoCal with drier air aloft, allowing \ntemperatures to warm both today and tomorrow to around 5-8 \ndegrees above normal. A trough off the backside of the larger low \nto the north will swing down late Saturday and attempt to capture \nsome of the moisture associated with Hurricane Polo and Hurricane \nOdalys, increasing PWs above 1-1.5\". This could lead to elevated \novernight temperatures both Saturday and Sunday for coastal areas \nand moderate HeatRisk is expected for Saturday. By Sunday, heavier\ncloud cover and decreasing heights will help moderate \ntemperatures a bit, which look to cool almost 10 degrees below \nSaturday's highs.\n\nRight now, GFS guidance attempts to bring in drier air on the \nbackside of the trough late weekend, which could shunt the \nmoisture to the east as the trough moves across, severely limiting\nprecipitation chances for Sunday night and Monday. The ECMWF\nremains the more wet model, indicating some light precipitation\nfor SoCal. With such disagreement in global models even 48-72 \nhours out, confidence in rainfall amounts in this forecast package\nremain very low. For now, the aforementioned trough may bring \nabout some light rain showers for at least the San Diego County \nMountains, Riverside County Mountains and lower deserts (including\nCoachella Valley), about a 20-25% chance, although any \naccumulation isn't expected to be very high, likely 0.2\" or less.\nEven if no accumulation occurs for our region, ample high clouds \nover nearly the entire region and mid-level moisture for the \nmountains and further east will likely lead to some uncomfortable \nhumidity. Outside of the humidity and low precipitation chances, \nsome other observable impacts associated with the tropical \ncyclones include coastal flooding and erosion, which is further \ndiscussed in the Beaches section below.\n\nAs the trough starts to propagate into Arizona on Monday, any \nlingering moisture will be pushed eastward as drier air filters in \nand ridging begins to build to the west, with PWs falling \nsharply. Temperatures will begin to warm, and weak offshore flow \nlooks to set up by mid to late week which could push temperatures \neven higher with dry downsloping winds. Since models remain \nmisaligned even in the short term, trying to dictate the upper \nlevel pattern after Wednesday is extremely difficult. Ultimately, \nhow the trough moves out and how quickly ridging build behind will\nplay a large impact in the general trend for the end of next \nweek.",
